WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.450
A fatal accident on the set of the Western
"Rust" shocked the world on October 21st,

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:09.440
as the movie's director of photography, Halyna
Hutchins, was fatally shot on location at

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.060
Bonanza Creek Ranch in Santa Fe, New Mexico.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:15.330
The movie's director, Joel Souza also received
injuries.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:19.210
Hutchins died at the University of New Mexico
Hospital, while actress Frances Fisher has

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:24.019
confirmed on Twitter that Souza is now out
of CHRISTUS St. Vincent Regional Medical Center.

00:00:24.019 --> 00:00:27.830
The news story is still developing and many
of the details surrounding the accident are

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:32.329
still unknown, but Hutchins and Souza received
their injuries after the movie's star and

00:00:32.329 --> 00:00:37.330
producer, Alec Baldwin, "discharged" a prop
firearm, which caused the incident in a yet-unconfirmed

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:38.330
way.

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:41.000
A representative of Santa Fe Sheriff's Department
has stated.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.020
"Mr. Baldwin was questioned by investigators
and released.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.280
No arrests or charges have been filed."

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:49.630
Early on October 22nd, Baldwin tweeted a statement
on the tragedy:

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:53.640
“There are no words to convey my shock and
sadness regarding the tragic accident that

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:58.370
took the life of Halyna Hutchins, a wife,
mother and deeply admired colleague of ours.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:02.690
I'm fully cooperating with the police investigation
to address how this tragedy occurred and I

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:05.929
am in touch with her husband, offering my
support to him and his family.

00:01:05.929 --> 00:01:10.020
My heart is broken for her husband, their
son, and all who knew and loved Halyna.”

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.890
So what, exactly, has happened?

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:16.369
Since the investigation is still ongoing and
many details of the incident are still unknown,

00:01:16.369 --> 00:01:20.159
it's too early to determine with certainty
what went wrong at Bonanza Creek Ranch.

00:01:20.159 --> 00:01:24.509
However, Alec Baldwin's spokesman has confirmed
that the weapon involved was a prop gun loaded

00:01:24.509 --> 00:01:25.549
with blank rounds.

00:01:25.549 --> 00:01:30.509
It is easy to assume that a blank round is
completely safe, but as BBC News notes, that

00:01:30.509 --> 00:01:32.350
couldn't be further from the truth.

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:36.179
Blanks are essentially regular rounds with
the projectile part at the tip removed and

00:01:36.179 --> 00:01:39.110
replaced with a comparatively harmless material,
like cotton.

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:42.979
However, the rest of the round still remains,
so when the gun is fired, the blank round

00:01:42.979 --> 00:01:47.000
still gives the same sonic and visual effect
as a live one — and even without a bullet

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.590
flying out, this means that the cartridge
still packs the kind of force that can send

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:51.810
bullets flying.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:55.500
If proper safety measurements aren't paid
attention to, this means a blank round could

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.289
very well be fatal.

00:01:57.289 --> 00:01:59.179
Accidents with prop guns have killed people
before.

00:01:59.179 --> 00:02:04.560
On March 31st, 1993, Bruce Lee's son Brandon
Lee died while filming "The Crow," when he

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.740
was shot in the abdomen with a prop gun that
had a dummy bullet stuck in front of the blank.

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:14.120
On October 12th, 1984, "Cover Up" star Jon-Erik
Hexum's intended joke went terribly wrong

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:18.090
when he shot himself in the temple with a
prop gun, not realizing that the blank round

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:20.010
was fatal at such a short distance.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:23.900
Because of the potential danger they pose,
prop guns should always be treated like real

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:24.900
ones.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.730
Prop department supervisor Kevin Williams
of UCLA School of Theater, Film and Television

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:29.730
told NPR,

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:33.409
"When I'm doing a training session with a
performer, my main guideline, the heartbeat

00:02:33.409 --> 00:02:37.010
of the conversation, is to make sure that
they are treating any weapon — whether it's

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:41.010
a bladed weapon or a firearm — as if it
could potentially kill somebody.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:42.989
And those are usually the words I use.

00:02:42.989 --> 00:02:46.000
It's a serious situation and everyone's got
to be on their game."

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.140
It's still unclear what circumstances led
to the events of the "Rust" accident, so it's

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:54.379
too early to say what the exact situation
was and whether all requisite preparations

00:02:54.379 --> 00:02:55.750
and precautions were followed.

00:02:55.750 --> 00:03:00.660
For now, the production of "Rust" has stopped,
as everyone involved is dealing with the tragedy.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:03.879
One life lost needlessly from a firearm is
one too many.

00:03:03.879 --> 00:03:08.250
Halyna Hutchins's life was cut tragically
short from the incident, and as more information

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:12.160
comes out regarding the incident, some are
already looking to take steps to ensure another

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:15.250
prop gun misfire never happens on a film set
ever again.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:19.700
In the aftermath of the tragedy, a Change.org
petition started with the goal of banning

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:21.430
prop firearms from movie shoots.

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:23.980
The mission statement for the petition reads
as follows:

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:26.360
"We need to make sure this never happens again.

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:29.830
There is no excuse for something like this
to happen in the 21st century.

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:32.709
Real guns are no longer needed on film production
sets."

00:03:32.709 --> 00:03:36.640
Others hope this tragedy serves as a teachable
lesson to other film productions planning

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.629
on using prop guns with blanks.

00:03:38.629 --> 00:03:42.650
Craig Zobel, a director known for his work
on "Mare of Easttown" and "The Leftovers,"

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:43.940
took to Twitter to exclaim,

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:47.959
"There's no reason to have guns loaded with
blanks or anything on set anymore.

00:03:47.959 --> 00:03:49.459
Should just be fully outlawed.

00:03:49.459 --> 00:03:50.569
There's computers now.

00:03:50.569 --> 00:03:53.040
The gunshots on Mare of Easttown are all digital.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:54.980
You can probably tell, but who cares?

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:56.480
It's an unnecessary risk."

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:01.000
As all involved deal with the fallout, here's
hoping another tragedy like this never happens

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:01.500
again.

